00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:17,680
Hey.

49
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:22,220
Recruiting is definitely the hardest part of a scoutmaster's job and the one I was least prepared for.

50
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:27,320
There's just not much training in the BSA for it, as if no one has figured it out.

51
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:29,660
I think you might be right there.

52
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,160
I asked, where do I find recruits?

53
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,360
The only answer was Weeblos.

54
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:39,160
And then I asked, can I go to their meeting and make a pitch for our troop?

55
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:43,140
And I was answered, all the Weeblos have crossed over by now.

56
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,120
So yeah, it's kind of like duck hunting.

57
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:51,820
If you're not there when the ducks are migrating, forget about it, right?

58
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,900
This is a daunting task for a troop of 10 scouts and three adults.

59
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:02,560
I have talked to boys who did not have a scouting background from church, youth groups, and sports teams

60
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,460
and have not seen any new scouts from these activities.

61
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:09,520
I would like to talk to the parents and explain how our program can help their children.

62
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:12,020
My question is, how do I get in front of them?

63
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:16,100
Well, Alan, I'm not too sure you want to be in front of them.

64
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:21,220
Because one of the points I'm trying to make about recruiting and the variable successes

65
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:26,540
that you have with different recruiting methods is that the wholesale methods don't seem to work real well.

66
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:32,019
And again, I don't have this all figured out, but I'm just going off the top of my head.

67
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,519
The wholesale methods, you talking in front of groups, don't seem to work too well.

68
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,680
You want retail.

69
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,959
Retail method, which is one-on-one, anytime that you possibly can.

70
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,660
So this is what I'm going to suggest to you.

71
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,180
How about this?

72
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:50,440
You have 10 scouts in the troop.

73
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:57,420
You say you have three leaders, but with 10 scouts, you have somewhere between 10 and 20 adults

74
00:03:57,420 --> 00:03:59,440
who are some way associated with the troop.

75
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,440
That includes the scouts' parents.

76
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:13,540
Now, can you motivate them to each talk to, you know, 5 or 10 other families within a given period of time about scouting?

77
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:20,180
If you do that, well, there's between 50 and 100 families, you know, 50 and 100 boys that you've approached about scouting.

78
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:27,140
I can almost guarantee, I can't positively guarantee, but I can almost guarantee if you do those numbers,

79
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:30,500
you're going to get somewhere between 2 to 10 scouts out of it.

80
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:32,700
If you get 10, you have double drew numbers.

81
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:36,740
If you get 2, that's 2 more than you've got now.

82
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:39,420
I mean, it's kind of hard work.

83
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:44,580
Naturally, the best thing to have is what I'm sure you already have,

84
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:51,680
which is an engaging, interesting, and wonderful program that advertises itself and draws boys in like a magnet.

85
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:55,720
But if you can motivate the folks to do that, and how would you do that?

86
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:59,620
Well, make up an invitation that has a series of dates on it.

87
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:05,360
Set aside one troop meeting every month as an open house meeting.

88
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:08,520
You don't have to change the program radically or anything like that.

89
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:10,799
Just, you know, note in your head that it's an open house,

90
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:13,479
and you're going to spend some time talking to whatever parents show up.

91
00:05:14,219 --> 00:05:15,840
And make that an invitation.

92
00:05:16,039 --> 00:05:17,820
Give it to all the parents in the troop.

93
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:20,620
You know, give them 10 copies apiece or whatever and say,

94
00:05:20,719 --> 00:05:22,799
hey, this is the way we're going to build our troop up.

95
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:23,599
It's going to be great.

96
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:28,659
Go ahead and give these to 10 families with a scout age boy

97
00:05:28,659 --> 00:05:33,719
and encourage them to come around to one of our open houses.

98
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:38,560
And you'll have, sometimes you'll have an open house and nobody will show up.

99
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:40,980
You'll have an open house and two people will show up.

100
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:45,560
And it will be alternatively encouraging and discouraging.

101
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:48,260
But that's basically the way of the world with this.

102
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:56,400
I would really like to hear from any of you listening out there about effective recruiting methods.

103
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:02,760
Maybe events or plans that you've tried that have worked out real well so we can share them with everybody else.

104
00:06:03,179 --> 00:06:03,900
Well, moving along.

105
00:06:04,419 --> 00:06:09,739
Today's podcast is a special about summer camp.

106
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:14,280
And in Scout Mastership, in seven minutes or less,

107
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:20,760
we're going to be talking about how to have a good week at summer camp as scout leaders.

108
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:28,680
And then in the second part of the show, we're going to talk a little bit about homesickness.

109
00:06:29,719 --> 00:06:30,200
Homesickness.

110
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:32,039
It's a real thing.

111
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:33,260
And it's something to deal with.

112
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:34,380
We got a full slate.

113
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,560
So let's get started, shall we?

114
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:44,940
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

115
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:59,760
So if you're fortunate enough to be able to spend a week at summer camp as a Scout Master,

116
00:07:00,099 --> 00:07:05,039
I want to give you a few ideas and reflections as a former camp director.

117
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,820
And I've got to tell you something.

118
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:11,219
Most of the difficulties we encountered were not caused by Scouts.

119
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:16,580
A lot of times where we ran into problems was with the leaders that the Scouts brought along with them.

120
00:07:16,580 --> 00:07:23,080
I want to acknowledge something very important, especially with the things that we're going to talk about here about camp.

121
00:07:23,419 --> 00:07:25,020
I'm no perfect guy.

122
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:31,080
I've been around long enough to see a few things and to acknowledge a few things, especially about myself.

123
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:34,680
So when I'm giving you this advice and these reflections,

124
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:41,740
it's not about me talking down to anybody or being whiny and complaining about things.

125
00:07:42,159 --> 00:07:45,299
You know, some of you have told me that these things help.

126
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:48,340
So I'm going to keep after it.

127
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:53,000
The first thing that I'll suggest, don't overdo it.

128
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:55,600
Personally, don't overdo it.

129
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,620
And don't make yourself into a time bomb.

130
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:01,420
Listen, if you get hot and tired and hungry and thirsty,

131
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:04,760
get cool and rested and fed and hydrated.

132
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:08,940
I tend to be really cranky if I don't look after myself.

133
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:15,740
And as the years go by, you know, I tend to get crankier a lot easier.

134
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:21,560
So years of observation prove that, you know, not only myself,

135
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:24,680
but some of my fellow leaders can have the same problem.

136
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:27,880
So don't wear yourself to a frazzle.

137
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:30,140
You know, look after yourself.

138
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:34,340
And in doing that, you'll be looking after the interests of your scouts

139
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:37,900
because you won't be a cranky, mean monster to them.

140
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:40,280
I've done it.

141
00:08:40,460 --> 00:08:41,000
I've done it.

142
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:43,919
So, you know, watch out for that one.

143
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:49,580
Camp staff members are totally dedicated to making your stay worthwhile.

144
00:08:49,699 --> 00:08:50,480
Believe it or not.

145
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:55,100
Younger staffers are learning at the same rate as your scouts.

146
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:58,180
And you need to be considerate and supportive of them.

147
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:02,420
Don't berate, criticize, or argue, or yell at a staff member or a youth leader

148
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:05,220
or a fellow adult leader in the presence of scouts,

149
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:07,880
no matter how much you think they might deserve it.

150
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:12,300
A quiet talk at an appropriate distance will usually solve the problem.

151
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:15,180
And the problem could turn out to be yours.

152
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,940
Don't pull rank while you're at camp.

153
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:19,200
Do you know what I'm saying?

154
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:20,140
You're a scoutmaster.

155
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:21,920
You're not a general or an admiral.

156
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,860
So there really isn't any rank to pull.

157
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:25,380
Right?

158
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:29,360
You know, most people instantly respect your position.

159
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,080
Members of staff are told to respect your position.

160
00:09:34,140 --> 00:09:35,960
Don't make a big deal out of it.

161
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:41,060
Don't ask for special privileges or try to impress staff members

162
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:43,620
with your own sense of self-importance.

163
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:45,700
I've been there before.

164
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:47,620
It's ugly.

165
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:52,040
Join in the program and encourage your scouts to do so,

166
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:54,660
even if the program, in your humble opinion, isn't all that good.

167
00:09:54,660 --> 00:10:01,719
Now, I know, you know, maybe you are a scout troop that goes back to the same camp year after year.

168
00:10:01,959 --> 00:10:03,319
That's my troop.

169
00:10:03,579 --> 00:10:06,920
We've been going to the same camp for probably, I would guess,

170
00:10:07,199 --> 00:10:10,199
since the troop was founded 65 years ago.

171
00:10:11,339 --> 00:10:16,180
Or maybe you're a visiting troop that circulates amongst different camps.

172
00:10:16,719 --> 00:10:18,680
You know, both are great options.

173
00:10:18,680 --> 00:10:21,239
I don't have an opinion as to which one is better.

174
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:25,739
But you're going to circulate through a camp or you're going to go back to your camp.

175
00:10:25,939 --> 00:10:29,699
And there's going to be one of those summers that's going to come along

176
00:10:29,699 --> 00:10:32,180
where it just seems like things are a little lackluster

177
00:10:32,180 --> 00:10:35,660
and they're not doing things as well as they did last year or at the other camp.

178
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:41,839
And, okay, so you recognize that and then you live with it

179
00:10:41,839 --> 00:10:49,819
and you say that you're going to go ahead and join in whatever program there is

180
00:10:49,819 --> 00:10:55,160
and make the best of it because a lively audience does more than anything

181
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:59,319
to enliven and energize a camp staff.

182
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:08,800
So when you get happy and involved, you'll see a commensurate response from the staff.

183
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:13,440
You know, participate in all the ceremonies and traditions and practices of the camp,

184
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:14,820
even if they're unfamiliar to you.

185
00:11:15,020 --> 00:11:17,360
Camps and camp staffs are proud of their traditions.

186
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:23,460
Imposing our way of doing things on the program as a scout leader

187
00:11:23,460 --> 00:11:28,260
is as rude as imposing your way of doing things in somebody else's home.

188
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:31,500
I mean, to a certain extent, you're a guest at your camp

189
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:34,680
and the staff is working hard to do things for you

190
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:37,460
and it's a good idea to play along with them.

191
00:11:38,740 --> 00:11:43,040
Carefully monitor any inner troop rivalries that may develop.

192
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:46,200
Now, as I said, we've been going back to the same camp for ages

193
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:50,100
and there are troops that show up there at the same time

194
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:54,200
and over time, sometimes a little rivalry can develop

195
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:59,680
and that can be healthy, but it also can be an unhealthy thing

196
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,360
to have happen during a week at camp.

197
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:04,540
Remember, scout age boys are hyper-competitive

198
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:09,060
and they often need to be reminded about fairness and courtesy and sportsmanship.

199
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,320
So set the tone of gentlemanly competition

200
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:15,320
and then require it of your scouts.

201
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:20,320
Give your youth leaders all the responsibility for success that week.

202
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:24,840
See that they understand what needs to be done and how and why

203
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:27,560
and then observe from a comfortable distance.

204
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:30,080
Check in with them every evening, ask lots of questions

205
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:32,600
and only put your oar in when it's asked

206
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:35,440
or, you know, it's a matter of safety or propriety.

207
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:36,580
Be proud of your scouts.

208
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:37,220
Encourage them.

209
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:38,060
Congratulate them.

210
00:12:38,180 --> 00:12:38,640
Admire them.

211
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:40,160
Don't try to be one of them

212
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:41,900
because you're not

213
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:44,020
and you won't be.

214
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:49,900
When the rare opportunity to join at the boy level presents itself,

215
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:52,040
when you get invited to do that,

216
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:53,939
jump in and have fun.

217
00:12:54,560 --> 00:12:56,579
But don't interfere with their fun.

218
00:12:57,480 --> 00:12:59,260
What is a successful week at camp?

219
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:01,800
That's largely for the troop to plan.

220
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:05,300
Yeah, the staff is there planning programs and things like that,

221
00:13:05,319 --> 00:13:06,520
but you also have to be thinking.

222
00:13:06,900 --> 00:13:09,420
I would tell you that a week at camp

223
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:13,979
is an unparalleled opportunity to encourage growth in your patrols.

224
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:17,680
Encourage them to more esprit de corps,

225
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:19,660
to more independent functioning.

226
00:13:20,079 --> 00:13:22,560
You place that responsibility on the patrol leaders.

227
00:13:22,699 --> 00:13:23,959
They should, they should,

228
00:13:24,520 --> 00:13:28,500
each patrol should sleep and eat and clean and work and play as a team.

229
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:33,060
Now, if your camp doesn't incentivize patrol excellence

230
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:34,380
with an award of some kind,

231
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:37,400
you can initiate one yourself or you can even add to it.

232
00:13:37,400 --> 00:13:41,900
You know, inspire a little friendly, gentlemanly competition amongst the troop.

233
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:47,459
It's also a fantastic opportunity to train your leaders.

234
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:53,720
You can capitalize on the week as on-the-job training for your youth leaders.

235
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:56,280
The difference between on-the-job training

236
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:58,819
and a more formal training thing

237
00:13:58,819 --> 00:14:03,020
is that the scouts are exercising leadership skills in actual time

238
00:14:03,020 --> 00:14:06,980
and in an actual situation instead of an artificial one.

239
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:10,819
Don't have instructional sessions that last a long time.

240
00:14:11,060 --> 00:14:11,720
Here's what you do.

241
00:14:12,040 --> 00:14:13,079
You get the guys together.

242
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:16,060
You give them five minutes on what's going to happen next,

243
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:17,720
the actions that they're going to need to take,

244
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:19,520
the plans that they're going to need to do,

245
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:21,199
and then let them go out and do it.

246
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:25,360
Then later on, get them back together for another five minutes,

247
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:28,840
five, F-I-V-E, not 10, not 15, not 20,

248
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:30,319
another five minutes,

249
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:31,620
and say, how did it go?

250
00:14:31,740 --> 00:14:33,940
What was unexpected that you encountered?

251
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:35,800
How did everybody perform?

252
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:38,040
And what can you improve on?

253
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:40,480
And if you make a habit of that,

254
00:14:40,520 --> 00:14:43,000
and you do that once a day for the week,

255
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:47,160
you're going to initiate a way of leadership training

256
00:14:47,160 --> 00:14:52,800
that is in that coaching mentoring model that we all want.

257
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:54,140
How about advancement?

258
00:14:54,580 --> 00:14:56,960
Frankly, I wish my scouts were more interested

259
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:59,319
in looking under rocks and climbing trees and going fishing

260
00:14:59,319 --> 00:15:02,340
than they were in sitting through merit badge sessions.

261
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:05,460
Nonetheless, they really like to earn merit badges,

262
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:09,000
and each scout should set an advancement goal for the week

263
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:10,480
with the assistance of their patrol leader.

264
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:12,699
That's a good way to prepare for camp.

265
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:15,699
There's more experiences to have at camp

266
00:15:15,699 --> 00:15:18,280
than just working on the merit badge system.

267
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:19,319
So there's some ideas,

268
00:15:19,439 --> 00:15:21,540
just some general ideas to throw out there

269
00:15:21,540 --> 00:15:23,959
to aid you in planning for camp

270
00:15:23,959 --> 00:15:25,939
and having a good experience once you get there.

271
00:15:25,940 --> 00:15:33,620
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

272
00:15:36,940 --> 00:15:40,500
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

273
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:47,420
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

274
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:50,800
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

275
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:54,220
You were always on the beat, boy, beat, boy.

276
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,680
Yeah, you were always on the beat, boy, beat, boy.

277
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,160
I'm hanging in the street, boy, street, boy.

278
00:16:01,580 --> 00:16:04,480
He was dancing to the beat, boy, beat, boy.

279
00:16:04,660 --> 00:16:07,940
Yeah, you're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

280
00:16:08,180 --> 00:16:11,380
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

281
00:16:12,280 --> 00:16:15,620
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

282
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:17,820
Night falls on our first day at summer camp

283
00:16:17,820 --> 00:16:19,600
after we've checked in,

284
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:21,520
after we've done our swim test,

285
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:23,120
we've gone to retreat,

286
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:23,980
we've gone to dinner,

287
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:25,800
we've gone to the big opening campfire.

288
00:16:26,439 --> 00:16:28,100
I'm sitting at my picnic table.

289
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:30,400
The Patrol Leaders' Council is meeting.

290
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:32,780
And as we're meeting,

291
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:36,480
at the outer perimeter of the lantern light

292
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:37,880
from our table,

293
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:41,020
I see a first-year Scout.

294
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:43,480
He doesn't quite come into the light.

295
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:44,939
He's just kind of orbiting out there.

296
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:48,040
He looks at us every once in a while,

297
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:48,880
kind of wistfully.

298
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:51,760
I know exactly why he's there.

299
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:54,740
He and his brethren have always been there.

300
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:57,640
For 20-plus years, they've been there.

301
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:00,920
He's a miniature, modern-day Odysseus

302
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:03,080
in the throes of some heroic ennui.

303
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:07,440
He is my homesick Scout.

304
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:12,060
Now, homesickness is very real.

305
00:17:12,060 --> 00:17:13,540
It can be light and passing,

306
00:17:13,879 --> 00:17:15,319
or it can be very dark and difficult.

307
00:17:16,419 --> 00:17:17,520
A lot of people,

308
00:17:17,639 --> 00:17:19,679
when they're separated from familiar surroundings

309
00:17:19,679 --> 00:17:23,139
or their family for an extended period of time,

310
00:17:23,419 --> 00:17:24,800
can become homesick.

311
00:17:25,859 --> 00:17:28,179
They can have a sense of dread or helplessness.

312
00:17:28,379 --> 00:17:29,260
They can be depressed.

313
00:17:29,419 --> 00:17:30,200
They can be sad.

314
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:31,179
They can be frustrated.

315
00:17:31,379 --> 00:17:32,200
They can be angry.

316
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:34,300
Or they can just feel kind of hopeless.

317
00:17:36,360 --> 00:17:37,419
Extreme cases

318
00:17:37,420 --> 00:17:40,760
even cause physical symptoms

319
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:42,880
like stomach pain and indigestion

320
00:17:42,880 --> 00:17:45,140
and headaches and nausea and tears.

321
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:50,160
You have to understand homesickness

322
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:51,200
as a Scoutmaster.

323
00:17:52,060 --> 00:17:53,720
It's relatively easy

324
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:55,680
to cure the vast majority of cases.

325
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:58,560
It's a very real problem,

326
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:00,500
and we shouldn't trivialize it.

327
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:01,880
I have to admit

328
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:02,620
that I've been guilty

329
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:04,180
of being less than sensitive

330
00:18:04,180 --> 00:18:06,500
in handling homesickness in the past,

331
00:18:06,760 --> 00:18:07,840
so let me share

332
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:09,720
some successful strategies with you.

333
00:18:12,020 --> 00:18:14,660
To alleviate homesickness

334
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:15,720
from the get-go,

335
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:18,940
making sure that parents understand

336
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:21,640
your expectations for camp

337
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:24,280
can be very helpful.

338
00:18:24,280 --> 00:18:25,740
Here's a sample

339
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:28,000
of what we put

340
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:29,840
into the materials

341
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:30,620
that we distribute

342
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:32,080
to our families

343
00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:33,740
before summer camp.

344
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:34,899
We say this.

345
00:18:35,200 --> 00:18:36,260
Parents are expected

346
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:38,020
to support their Scout's commitment

347
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:39,700
to spend a full week at camp.

348
00:18:40,639 --> 00:18:41,639
There is a full schedule

349
00:18:41,639 --> 00:18:42,920
of shared responsibilities

350
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:44,960
that begins on the Sunday we arrive

351
00:18:44,960 --> 00:18:46,360
and continues unbroken

352
00:18:46,360 --> 00:18:48,120
until we leave a week later.

353
00:18:48,840 --> 00:18:50,520
If a Scout must arrive late,

354
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:51,399
leave early,

355
00:18:51,520 --> 00:18:52,800
or spend time out of camp

356
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:53,500
during the week,

357
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:54,960
please let us know

358
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:55,920
as early as possible

359
00:18:55,920 --> 00:18:57,840
so corresponding arrangements

360
00:18:57,840 --> 00:18:58,700
can be made.

361
00:18:59,060 --> 00:18:59,980
That being said,

362
00:19:00,680 --> 00:19:01,460
arriving late,

363
00:19:01,780 --> 00:19:02,360
leaving early,

364
00:19:02,440 --> 00:19:03,420
or spending time away

365
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:04,580
from camp during the week

366
00:19:04,580 --> 00:19:05,860
is actively discouraged

367
00:19:05,860 --> 00:19:06,980
as it tends to compromise

368
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:07,700
the experience

369
00:19:07,700 --> 00:19:09,820
not only for the individual Scout,

370
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:11,120
but for the rest of his troop

371
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:11,660
and patrol.

372
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:13,700
A week of camp

373
00:19:13,700 --> 00:19:15,340
is often the longest time

374
00:19:15,340 --> 00:19:16,600
our first-year Scouts

375
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:17,380
have spent away

376
00:19:17,380 --> 00:19:18,360
from home and family.

377
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:20,340
It's natural that some Scouts

378
00:19:20,340 --> 00:19:21,380
find this experience

379
00:19:21,380 --> 00:19:22,480
difficult to endure,

380
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:24,240
as do their parents.

381
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:25,980
Our experience

382
00:19:25,980 --> 00:19:26,940
with hundreds of Scouts

383
00:19:26,940 --> 00:19:27,480
and parents

384
00:19:27,480 --> 00:19:28,420
of all temperaments

385
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:29,100
assures us

386
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:30,760
that they not only endure

387
00:19:30,760 --> 00:19:31,920
but flourish

388
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:33,020
and return home

389
00:19:33,020 --> 00:19:34,180
having gained a great deal

390
00:19:34,180 --> 00:19:35,220
from the experience.

391
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:38,360
Now, what do the experts

392
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:38,900
recommend?

393
00:19:39,020 --> 00:19:39,860
Because homesickness

394
00:19:39,860 --> 00:19:41,040
is not limited to Scouts.

395
00:19:41,900 --> 00:19:42,600
There is actually,

396
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:43,040
you know,

397
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:44,320
a reasonable amount

398
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:44,960
of homesickness

399
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:45,560
that happens

400
00:19:45,560 --> 00:19:46,360
when people go away

401
00:19:46,360 --> 00:19:46,940
to college

402
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:48,240
and it can be

403
00:19:48,240 --> 00:19:49,200
kind of debilitating.

404
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:51,580
So, you know,

405
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:52,800
people study this

406
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:54,300
and people try

407
00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:55,760
and alleviate

408
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:57,000
the problem

409
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,180
and counselors

410
00:19:59,180 --> 00:20:00,000
work on this

411
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:00,640
quite a bit

412
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:01,840
even with adults.

413
00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:04,800
So, here's

414
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:05,379
what they recommend.

415
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:06,220
Sometimes bringing

416
00:20:06,220 --> 00:20:07,320
a couple of mementos

417
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:07,899
from home

418
00:20:07,899 --> 00:20:08,800
such as, you know,

419
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:09,919
photos or something

420
00:20:09,919 --> 00:20:10,419
like that

421
00:20:10,419 --> 00:20:11,240
can serve as a

422
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:12,199
transitional object

423
00:20:12,199 --> 00:20:13,399
that help relieve

424
00:20:13,399 --> 00:20:15,020
uncomfortable feelings.

425
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:17,760
I've had boys

426
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:18,280
bring a little

427
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:18,900
stuffed animal

428
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:19,400
with them

429
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:21,300
and, you know,

430
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:22,180
we're on notice

431
00:20:22,180 --> 00:20:23,460
as a troop

432
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:24,080
and as adults.

433
00:20:24,200 --> 00:20:25,180
We don't make fun

434
00:20:25,180 --> 00:20:25,420
of that.

435
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:26,600
We don't trivialize that.

436
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:28,460
If they need to bring

437
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:29,480
that little stuffed animal

438
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:30,420
or a photo

439
00:20:30,420 --> 00:20:31,240
or something like that,

440
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:31,800
that's fine.

441
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:33,980
Now, you might

442
00:20:33,980 --> 00:20:35,520
be grimacing right now

443
00:20:35,520 --> 00:20:35,879
and saying,

444
00:20:36,020 --> 00:20:36,720
gee, that seems

445
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,000
awfully namby-pamby

446
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:39,200
for a boy

447
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:40,560
who's 11 or 12 years old.

448
00:20:40,660 --> 00:20:42,240
Well, if it helps

449
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:43,060
them get through,

450
00:20:43,060 --> 00:20:45,020
I think it's useful.

451
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:46,500
It doesn't weaken them.

452
00:20:47,060 --> 00:20:48,040
It doesn't, you know,

453
00:20:48,159 --> 00:20:48,960
it doesn't impinge

454
00:20:48,960 --> 00:20:49,659
on the experience

455
00:20:49,659 --> 00:20:50,220
that they're having

456
00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:50,639
at camp.

457
00:20:50,940 --> 00:20:51,760
It helps them

458
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:52,899
move through

459
00:20:52,899 --> 00:20:55,320
those uncomfortable

460
00:20:55,320 --> 00:20:56,679
feelings of homesickness.

461
00:20:57,659 --> 00:20:58,580
So, how do we treat it?

462
00:20:58,919 --> 00:20:59,560
How do we treat

463
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:00,200
homesickness?

464
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:01,700
Well, talking is usually

465
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:03,399
a very strong way

466
00:21:03,399 --> 00:21:04,860
of treating homesickness.

467
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:06,520
It involves,

468
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:07,840
since there are

469
00:21:07,840 --> 00:21:08,639
feelings of dread

470
00:21:08,639 --> 00:21:09,840
and helplessness involved

471
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:12,520
and they're

472
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:13,660
mostly baseless.

473
00:21:14,860 --> 00:21:15,040
You know,

474
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:16,940
talking can often

475
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:17,900
make these,

476
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:20,120
that particular case

477
00:21:20,120 --> 00:21:21,120
of homesickness

478
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:21,500
dissolve.

479
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:24,160
Staying active

480
00:21:24,160 --> 00:21:24,879
and involved

481
00:21:24,879 --> 00:21:25,760
is always important.

482
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:26,580
Homesick scouts

483
00:21:26,580 --> 00:21:27,440
are often reluctant

484
00:21:27,440 --> 00:21:28,160
to participate

485
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:28,980
in much of anything

486
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:29,840
because they're kind of

487
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:30,960
perseverating on the fact

488
00:21:30,960 --> 00:21:31,800
that they're homesick.

489
00:21:32,680 --> 00:21:34,160
So, we've got to

490
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:34,800
make every effort

491
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:35,740
to keep them active.

492
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:38,680
staying engaged

493
00:21:38,680 --> 00:21:40,660
is also

494
00:21:40,660 --> 00:21:42,000
a very important

495
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:43,200
cure to homesickness.

496
00:21:43,620 --> 00:21:45,040
Lots of group activities

497
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:46,400
and shared responsibilities.

498
00:21:46,720 --> 00:21:47,480
You don't want scouts

499
00:21:47,480 --> 00:21:48,140
just to be off

500
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:48,620
in a corner.

501
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:49,420
Like I say,

502
00:21:49,560 --> 00:21:50,740
perseverating on the fact

503
00:21:50,740 --> 00:21:51,640
that I'm homesick

504
00:21:51,640 --> 00:21:52,900
and I miss mom and dad

505
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:54,360
and I'd really rather

506
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:55,420
be home than be here.

507
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:57,020
They need to stay

508
00:21:57,020 --> 00:21:58,140
with other guys

509
00:21:58,140 --> 00:21:59,220
and they need to be involved

510
00:21:59,220 --> 00:22:00,200
with other guys.

511
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:02,180
I often ask

512
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:03,240
an older scout

513
00:22:03,240 --> 00:22:05,000
that has experienced

514
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:06,360
homesickness in the past

515
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:08,500
to go and engage

516
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:09,760
with a newer scout

517
00:22:09,760 --> 00:22:10,740
who's feeling

518
00:22:10,740 --> 00:22:11,560
a little homesick

519
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:12,080
and that usually

520
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:12,840
does the trick.

521
00:22:13,960 --> 00:22:14,840
Writing or calling home.

522
00:22:14,920 --> 00:22:15,760
Now, I used to think

523
00:22:15,760 --> 00:22:17,000
that this was anathema.

524
00:22:17,140 --> 00:22:17,700
I used to think

525
00:22:17,700 --> 00:22:18,740
that this was the worst

526
00:22:18,740 --> 00:22:19,760
thing that you could do

527
00:22:19,760 --> 00:22:21,880
because once they called home

528
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:23,440
they would, you know,

529
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:24,940
arrange some way

530
00:22:24,940 --> 00:22:26,700
of making their parents

531
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:27,600
feel so bad

532
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:28,660
that they would come

533
00:22:28,660 --> 00:22:29,300
and pick them up.

534
00:22:29,300 --> 00:22:30,700
They would go away

535
00:22:30,700 --> 00:22:31,159
from camp

536
00:22:31,159 --> 00:22:31,639
and we'd probably

537
00:22:31,639 --> 00:22:32,180
never see them

538
00:22:32,180 --> 00:22:32,879
in scouts again.

539
00:22:34,200 --> 00:22:35,480
But writing or calling home

540
00:22:35,480 --> 00:22:36,379
can be very helpful.

541
00:22:37,320 --> 00:22:38,760
Actually, speaking to someone

542
00:22:38,760 --> 00:22:40,360
is probably one of the

543
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:41,399
more helpful things

544
00:22:41,399 --> 00:22:42,100
that can happen.

545
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:44,100
This is what I do.

546
00:22:44,159 --> 00:22:45,960
Before we make any calls home,

547
00:22:46,659 --> 00:22:48,080
I speak to parents first.

548
00:22:49,340 --> 00:22:50,940
And the first words

549
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:51,740
out of my mouth is,

550
00:22:51,840 --> 00:22:52,659
hey, you know,

551
00:22:52,720 --> 00:22:53,300
this is Clark

552
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:54,280
and I'm down at camp

553
00:22:54,280 --> 00:22:56,180
and everything is fine.

554
00:22:56,600 --> 00:22:57,639
He's not hurt.

555
00:22:57,640 --> 00:22:59,400
He's just feeling

556
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:00,140
a little lowgy

557
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:01,160
and I want you

558
00:23:01,160 --> 00:23:02,560
to be able to talk to him

559
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,020
and encourage him

560
00:23:04,020 --> 00:23:05,400
to stick this out

561
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:06,020
because, you know,

562
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:07,120
he's going to be just fine.

563
00:23:08,300 --> 00:23:09,340
And parents can be

564
00:23:09,340 --> 00:23:10,360
as worried as the boys

565
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:10,880
sometimes.

566
00:23:11,520 --> 00:23:12,320
So I like to talk

567
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:12,880
to them first

568
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:13,600
before the scout

569
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:14,320
talks to them.

570
00:23:16,460 --> 00:23:16,640
You know,

571
00:23:16,660 --> 00:23:17,440
college counselors

572
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:18,060
and chaplains,

573
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:18,660
as I said,

574
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:20,200
report that there are

575
00:23:20,200 --> 00:23:21,240
a lot of freshman students

576
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:21,920
that experience

577
00:23:21,920 --> 00:23:22,620
homesickness

578
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:23,740
serious enough

579
00:23:23,740 --> 00:23:26,140
to affect their studies.

580
00:23:26,140 --> 00:23:28,500
And if scouts

581
00:23:28,500 --> 00:23:29,320
can overcome

582
00:23:29,320 --> 00:23:30,140
homesickness

583
00:23:30,140 --> 00:23:31,140
during that week

584
00:23:31,140 --> 00:23:31,900
at summer camp

585
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:33,360
at an earlier age,

586
00:23:33,660 --> 00:23:34,740
they'll be better prepared

587
00:23:34,740 --> 00:23:35,800
for those experiences.

588
00:23:36,980 --> 00:23:38,560
So we mustn't

589
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:40,440
trivialize homesickness.

590
00:23:41,100 --> 00:23:41,980
We mustn't

591
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:43,720
berate boys

592
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:44,700
for being homesick,

593
00:23:45,020 --> 00:23:46,020
make them feel worse.

594
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:47,740
If we do a little counseling,

595
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:48,460
a little mentoring,

596
00:23:48,620 --> 00:23:49,240
a little coaching,

597
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:51,060
it'll solve

598
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:52,060
the vast majority

599
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:52,620
of cases

600
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:53,600
in just a couple

601
00:23:53,600 --> 00:23:54,060
of minutes

602
00:23:54,060 --> 00:23:54,840
and everything

603
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:55,460
will be fine.

